{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Jacob called to his sons.</b> They said in the Midrash: Jacob began his blessing from the place where his father Isaac left off blessing him, as it is said (Genesis 28:1): ״ויקרא יצחק אל יעקב ויברך אותו״. And Moses began (Deuteronomy 33:1): ״וזאת הברכה״ — from the place where Jacob left off his blessings. And David began the book of Psalms with ״אשרי האיש״ from the place where Moses left off his blessings (Deuteronomy 33:29): ״אשריך ישראל מי כמוך עם נושע בה׳״. And of this David said (Psalms 119): ״מזקנים אתבונן״." "<b>In the end of days.</b> These are the days of the Messiah. And it would have been fitting for him to tell them future matters concerning the near redemption, which is the redemption from Egypt — and how is it possible that he would leave aside the near matter and not allude to that wondrous redemption, and allude instead to the far-off redemption destined to come about in the end of days?" "<b>And it is possible</b> to explain that he had already alluded to the redemption from Egypt above, in the midst of his words: for what he said, ״והיה אלהים עמכם״, alluded to them regarding the exile of Egypt, and what he said, ״והשיב אתכם אל ארץ אבותיכם״ — this is the redemption from Egypt, for Israel were worthy of entering the land immediately upon their departure from Egypt, were it not for the sin of the spies that delayed them in the wilderness forty years. And after he alluded to the redemption of Egypt, he added an allusion to this final redemption in the end of days, because the two resemble one another, and it is a tradition in the hands of the prophets that our redemption, this one, is destined to be in the likeness of the redemption from Egypt in many respects, besides that there will be in it an added excellence and attainment in the hearts, in the knowledge of the Holy One, blessed be He. And he used in it two expressions, which are האספו and הקבצו, to allude to two gatherings: they are נדחי ישראל (the outcasts of Israel) and נפוצות יהודה (the dispersed of Judah), after the verse (Isaiah 11:12): ״ואסף נדחי ישראל ונפוצות יהודה יקבץ״. And Scripture also says (ibid. 56): ״עוד אקבץ עליו לנקבציו״ — that is, I will yet gather upon him, to those already gathered; this is the tribe of Judah and Benjamin, upon those gathered ones, who are the ten tribes. For we do not find a gathering of all the exile of Israel except in the redemption from the land of Egypt and in this future redemption. And of this Scripture says (ibid. 11): ״יוסיף ה׳ שנית ידו לקנות את שאר עמו״ — it says שנית, a second time, corresponding to the redemption of Israel from Egypt, where all Israel were gathered; for in the redemption of Babylon they were not all gathered, for only two and four myriad (i.e., twenty-four thousand) went up from Babylon, and most of Israel were exiled to Halah and Habor, the river of Gozan, and the cities of Media, and they never returned. And of this Scripture says שנית — a second time."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:22.017372779Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}